## 1. Understand the systems

| System | Responsibility | What you verify |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Approved application template | Generates a starting application for the selected platform | The generated project—not the template generator itself—is committed to your source repository. |
| Application source repository | Stores the application, dependency locks, tests, native configuration, and declared metadata | Repository identity, required source branch, and exact source commit. |
| Platform configuration repository | Stores secret-free enrollment requests and their review history | Request location, schema, selected template version, access groups, and promotion commits. |
| Approved workflow catalog | Defines the available immutable CI/CD templates and their compatibility rules | The selected template version is currently approved for the requested platform and routes. |
| Vela / protected apply automation | Validates requests and, at the protected live boundary, performs authorized provisioning and orchestration | Validation versus live-apply mode, exact commit, correlation identity, and sanitized outcome. |
| Bitrise | Executes builds and produces private artifacts, reports, and build evidence | Workflow/route identity, source commit, build status, and produced artifacts. |
| Sauce Labs | Executes the configured real-device tests | Exact tested artifact, device/test configuration, outcomes, and retained evidence. |
| NowSecure | Performs the configured application-security assessment | Assessment completion, applicable findings, and the release-gate decision. |
| App Management | Performs the approved internal distribution process, including distribution signing where applicable | Exact version, signing outcome, enablement, audience assignment, and catalog visibility. |
| Google Play / App Store Connect | Processes public-store testing and release submissions | Destination build/version, processing and release state, and intended tester/user availability. |

Internal endpoints, access-group names, application IDs, and operational links belong in the approved access-controlled location. Examples in this guide use placeholders rather than real identifiers or credentials.

